# Cloudflare Pages redirects.
#
# The Netlify twin of `apps/web/public/_redirects`, for the Cloudflare
# migration. It lives here rather than in `public/` on purpose: anything in
# `public/` is copied into the static export and served at a public URL, and
# shipping a second redirects file as a downloadable asset is clutter nobody
# asked for.
#
# To deploy to Cloudflare, copy this over `apps/web/public/_redirects` (or
# drop it into the build output) and set `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL`.
#
# THE API PROXY CANNOT BE PORTED. Cloudflare Pages documents that proxying
# "will only support relative URLs on your site. You cannot proxy external
# domains", so Netlify's
#
#   /api/* https://runrealm.coupondj.fun/api/:splat 200
#
# has no equivalent here. Two honest options, and this repo takes the second:
#
#   1. Put the API on the same Pages zone behind a Worker route, so the
#      proxy becomes same-origin. More moving parts, and it puts the API and
#      the static site on one hostname — which is the coupling this migration
#      is trying to remove.
#   2. Set NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL to the API's absolute origin and let the
#      browser call it directly. The server already sends
#      `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` in both netlify.toml and server.js,
#      so this needs no server change at all.
#
# Option 2 is what `ConfigService.apiUrl()` implements, and it is the only
# reason this file can be this short.
#
# Do NOT add `/api/* /index.html 200` here to "handle" API calls on a static
# host. With no `/api/*` rule, requests fall through to the SPA fallback
# below and get index.html with a 200 — which reads as a successful API
# response and is not one. Better to 404.

# SPA fallback — serve index.html for every other route. A static export has
# three real routes; anything else is client-side navigation, and answering
# 404 here would break a deep link and a refresh on it.
/* /index.html 200
